// In the previous chapter we saw that $rho$ sat low, inside the range of pooling regime. This does not mean that no signalling emerged; it means only that the token carries little information about a driver's hidden type. Which leaves a sharper question: did the agents signal at all, and if so, in what sense? Our answer, and the thread that runs through this chapter, is that weak type-associated signalling did emerge, in a sense the field's standard metrics are not built to detect: senders commit to a token pattern deliberately, receivers act on the token they observe, and the population comes to rely on the resulting token-behaviour coupling, enough that corrupting it raises collisions substantially. We read this as a convention of _use_, a shared regularity in how the token is emitted and read, rather than a convention of _meaning about type_, which is exactly what did not emerge.

The previous chapter left an odd pairing unresolved: a token that reveals almost nothing about a driver's type, yet whose disruption the population pays for dearly. This chapter reads that pairing through the game theory of signalling and convention.

Driving on a highway can be read as a coordination problem. In Lewis's terms @lewis1969convention[p.~24], it is a decision-making situation involving two or more agents whose interests mostly coincide and who can coordinate on at least two different equilibrium outcomes. Concretely, there are many mutually compatible ways to interleave traffic, such as who leads a merge and who holds a lane. Drivers only need to settle on one of them, since almost any consistent choice works, while a mismatch ends in a collision. It is worth noting that such a game is not pure coordination. Coordinating still leaves "who yields, who takes the gap, who leads the merge". The yielder loses time; the one who goes gains. So drivers agree they must coordinate but disagree which equilibrium they prefer. That is a mixed-motive game in the sense of @schelling1960strategy[p:p.~89]. Highway driving is thus coordination-dominant but not pure coordination, mixed-motive at the edges.

What a message is worth in such a game depends on what one takes a signal to be for, and here game theory offers two quite different accounts. In the first account, a signal reveals something private about the sender. This is the logic of costly signalling @spence1973job and of cheap talk @crawford1982strategic, and it is what the separation coefficient $rho$ measures. Our channel is not cheap talk in the strict sense. Emitting a token selects an execution profile, so it changes the sender's own kinematics, and therefore its payoff, whether or not any neighbour ever reads it. That places it closer to the costly-signalling side of the pair, and it is the reason the binding, rather than the symbol, turns out to be what matters. Judged this way, the agents barely communicate at all, since the token carries less than a tenth of a bit about a driver's hidden type. In the second account a signal need reveal nothing. Its only job is to let everyone settle on the same arbitrary convention out of the many that would serve equally well, in the sense of @lewis1969convention[p:pp.~16-24] and @schelling1960strategy[p:pp.~54-58].

A coordination problem is not solely solved by revealing private information; it is solved by everyone landing on the same rule. For that reason our results read more naturally on the side of the second account, even though the metrics the field reaches for by default assume the first. The token says almost nothing about a driver's hidden type, yet the whole population organises its driving around it. It is not a failed code, it is what a convention looks like when the game rewards agreement over disclosure.

It helps to be precise about what kind of convention this is. A token can carry two very different conventions. The first is a _convention of meaning_ about type, where a token comes to stand for cautious or assertive so that reading it tells you who the sender is. This is what $rho$ scores, and it is exactly what did not emerge. The second is a _convention of use_: a shared, self-consistent regularity in how the population emits the token and how receivers respond to it, whatever it reveals about type. It is this second convention that emerged. Senders commit to a token pattern deliberately (Speaker Consistency well above chance), and receivers act on the token they observe (non-zero decoding in every one of the twenty seeds). The population comes to rely on the resulting token-to-behaviour coupling, enough that corrupting it raises collisions by 65% to 86%. What emerged is a habit of use that coordinates driving, not a code that reveals type. We encourage readers to review example (1) Ship Flag Hoists and (2) The Trucker and The Driver (titles added by the authors) in @lewis1969convention[pp.~125-127, 129-130] for illustration of _convention of use_.

That the channel is a convention rather than a code shows first in how the token is used from one run to the next. @fig-sig-heatmap shows that, on average, both types place most of their weight on token 2 (assertive kinematic envelope) though the preference is weak. The per-seed breakdown in @fig-sig-heatmap-seed tells the fuller story: individual seeds settle on different token conventions, and among the well-performing seeds several distinct choices reach comparable return. Different seeds assign the tokens differently, cautious agents commit to token 0 in one seed and to token 1 in another, yet both drive equally well. Relabelling the tokens in one seed's solution and it behaves like another seed's. No single labelling is privileged because the task never implicitly specifies which integer should mean which type, only that whichever mapping a population settles on stays consistent. The sharply higher collision rate and lower mean return under _permute_, _desync_ and _randomise_ are what a mismatch in a coordination problem looks like: sender and receiver are operating on different conventions, and the outcome degrades accordingly.

